The code statements that lead to the bug are:
sock_orphan(sk);
sock_put(sk);
Reason: These lines were executed without first setting the SEND_SHUTDOWN flag, canceling the pending tx_work, or purging the write queue. Consequently, rawsock_tx_work could continue running after the socket was orphaned and the NCI device potentially freed, leading to use-after-free or leaked references.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (sk->sk_state == TCP_ESTABLISHED) {
    sk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN;
    cancel_work_sync(&nfc_rawsock(sk)->tx_work);
    rawsock_write_queue_purge(sk);
}
Reason: Setting SEND_SHUTDOWN prevents rawsock_tx_work from starting new transmits (as the work routine checks this flag). cancel_work_sync waits for any in‑progress execution to finish, ensuring no further use of the NCI device after teardown. Purging the write queue removes any remaining skbs, preventing resource leaks.

